.TH ZIG_VARIABLES 7 "2026-10-06" "Zig 0.17.0" "Zig Manual"
.SH NAME
zig-variables \- variable declarations, mutability, and storage in Zig
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
[\fIpub\fR] [\fIextern\fR | \fIexport\fR] [\fIthreadlocal\fR] [\fIcomptime\fR]
    (\fBconst\fR | \fBvar\fR) \fIname\fR [\fB:\fR \fIType\fR] \fB=\fR \fIinit\fR \fB;\fR
.fi
.PP
The type annotation is optional when the initializer determines the type.
The initializer is mandatory. Declarations without an initializer are
rejected by the parser.
.SH DESCRIPTION
Zig has exactly two declaration keywords: \fBconst\fR and \fBvar\fR.
\fBconst\fR creates an immutable binding; \fBvar\fR creates a mutable one.
There is no \fBlet\fR, \fBfinal\fR, \fBmutable\fR, or \fBimmutable\fR.
.PP
A declaration fixes three independent properties at the point of writing:
.TP
\fBMutability\fR
Set by the keyword (\fBconst\fR or \fBvar\fR).
.TP
\fBKnowability\fR
Set by whether the initializer is compile-time constant. A \fBcomptime\fR
modifier forces compile-time evaluation.
.TP
\fBStorage duration\fR
Set by the declaration site: local, namespace-level, function-scoped global,
thread-local, or comptime-only.
.PP
The compiler does not infer mutability from usage, does not guess storage
class, and does not defer binding decisions to link time except for
\fBextern\fR symbols.
.SH DECLARATIONS
.SS const
.nf
    const name = init;
    const name: Type = init;
.fi
.PP
The binding cannot be reassigned. Assignment to a \fBconst\fR name is a
compile error:
.nf
    const x = 10;
    x = 20;   // error: cannot assign to constant
.fi
.PP
\fBconst\fR applies to the binding, not to the pointee. A \fBconst\fR
pointer to non-const data may modify the pointed-to value:
.nf
    const p: *u8 = &byte;
    p.* = 42;   // ok
.fi
.PP
To make the pointee immutable, qualify the pointed-to type:
.nf
    const p: *const u8 = &byte;
    p.* = 42;   // error: cannot assign to dereference of const pointer
.fi
.SS var
.nf
    var name = init;
    var name: Type = init;
.fi
.PP
The binding may be reassigned for the duration of its lifetime. Zig rejects
a \fBvar\fR that is never mutated:
.nf
    var x: i32 = 5;   // error: local variable is never mutated
.fi
.PP
Discard the binding explicitly when mutation is not required:
.nf
    var x: i32 = 5;
    _ = &x;
.fi
.PP
The same rule applies to unused \fBconst\fR bindings
(\fBunused local variable\fR).
.SS Modifiers
.TP
\fBcomptime\fR
Forces the value to be computed during semantic analysis. See the
\fBCOMPTIME\fR section.
.TP
\fBthreadlocal\fR
Each thread receives an independent instance. Valid only at namespace
level. Restricted to \fBvar\fR. See the \fBSTORAGE DURATION\fR section.
.TP
\fBextern\fR
The symbol is provided by another object at link time.
.TP
\fBexport\fR
The symbol is published to the linker with a C-ABI-compatible type.
.TP
\fBpub\fR
Makes a namespace-level declaration visible outside its containing
namespace.
.SH INITIALIZATION
Every variable must be initialized at the point of declaration.
Declarations without an initializer are rejected by the parser:
.nf
    var x: i32;   // error: expected '=', found ';'
.fi
.PP
When a value is not yet available, initialize with \fBundefined\fR.
.SH UNDEFINED
.nf
    var x: T = undefined;
.fi
.PP
\fBundefined\fR marks memory with no meaningful value. Reading it before
writing is illegal behavior:
.IP \(bu 2
In \fBDebug\fR and \fBReleaseSafe\fR, the memory is filled with \fB0xaa\fR
and reads are checked at runtime.
.IP \(bu 2
In \fBReleaseFast\fR and \fBReleaseSmall\fR, the contents are unspecified
and the optimizer may assume the memory is never read.
.PP
\fBundefined\fR is not \fBnull\fR. \fBnull\fR is a value of an optional
type. \fBundefined\fR is the absence of a value.
.PP
\fBundefined\fR is not zero. The runtime representation is unspecified.
.PP
\fBundefined\fR coerces to any type, which makes it usable as a placeholder
in generic code.
.SH SCOPE AND SHADOWING
An identifier refers to at most one binding within a scope. Zig rejects
shadowing:
.nf
    const x = 10;
    fn f() void {
        const x = 20;   // error: local variable shadows declaration
    }
.fi
.PP
Declarations in disjoint scopes may reuse the same name:
.nf
    fn f() void {
        { const x = 1; _ = x; }
        { const x = 2; _ = x; }
    }
.fi
.PP
Field names are resolved through the type, not through the lexical scope,
and do not participate in shadowing.
.SH STORAGE DURATION
.SS Local variables
Declared inside a function, comptime block, or labeled block. Stored on the
stack unless the compiler assigns a register. Taking the address forces a
stack slot. The address is invalid after the enclosing block returns:
.nf
    fn f() *i32 {
        var x: i32 = 42;
        return &x;   // error: returning address of local variable
    }
.fi
.PP
A local \fBconst\fR is not necessarily comptime-known. It is comptime-known
only when its initializer is:
.nf
    fn f(n: usize) void {
        const a = 42;      // comptime-known
        const b = n * 2;   // runtime-known
    }
.fi
.SS Namespace-level variables
Declared at file scope or inside a struct, union, enum, or opaque type.
Stored in the global data section. The address is fixed at link time and
remains valid for the program's lifetime.
.PP
Namespace-level declarations are order-independent and lazily analyzed.
Unreferenced declarations are never analyzed, and compile errors they
contain are never reported:
.nf
    const unused = @compileError("never fires");   // ok
    const used   = @compileError("fires");         // error
.fi
.PP
Namespace-level initializers are implicitly \fBcomptime\fR when possible.
.SS Function-scoped globals
Zig has no \fBstatic\fR keyword. A namespace declared inside a function
serves the same purpose:
.nf
    fn counter() u32 {
        const S = struct { var count: u32 = 0; };
        S.count += 1;
        return S.count;
    }
.fi
.PP
\fBS.count\fR lives in the global data section and persists across calls.
The pattern is not thread-safe.
.SS Thread-local variables
.nf
    threadlocal var name: T = init;
.fi
.PP
Each thread receives an independent instance. The initializer must be
comptime-known. Valid only at namespace level.
.PP
Constraints:
.IP \(bu 2
\fBthreadlocal const\fR is rejected. \fBconst\fR bindings are inlined and
have no storage to localize.
.IP \(bu 2
Declarations inside functions are rejected. Thread-local storage is
allocated at thread creation.
.IP \(bu 2
The initializer must be a compile-time constant.
.PP
With \fB-fsingle-threaded\fR, \fBthreadlocal\fR is treated as an ordinary
namespace-level binding.
.SS Comptime variables
.nf
    comptime var name = init;
.fi
.PP
Computed and stored entirely during semantic analysis. No runtime storage
is allocated. See the \fBCOMPTIME\fR section.
.SH POINTERS AND MUTABILITY
Four combinations of pointer and pointee mutability:
.nf
    *T                pointer mutable, pointee mutable
    *const T          pointer mutable, pointee immutable
    const *T          pointer immutable, pointee mutable
    const *const T    pointer immutable, pointee immutable
.fi
.PP
\fBconst\fR attaches to the item written immediately after it.
.SH COMPTIME
\fBcomptime\fR forces evaluation during semantic analysis. It can appear as
a modifier on a declaration, as a parameter qualifier, or as a block:
.nf
    fn max(comptime T: type, a: T, b: T) T {
        return if (b > a) b else a;
    }

    comptime {
        var sum: u32 = 0;
        for (1..10) |i| sum += i;
    }
.fi
.PP
Properties:
.IP \(bu 2
The value is fully determined at compile time.
.IP \(bu 2
Runtime values cannot be stored into a \fBcomptime var\fR.
.IP \(bu 2
All control flow inside a comptime context is evaluated at compile time.
.IP \(bu 2
The value may participate in type construction.
.PP
A \fBcomptime var\fR exists only during compilation. It is not present in
the emitted binary.
.SH LIFETIME AND OWNERSHIP
Reading or writing memory after its lifetime ends is illegal behavior.
Three lifetime categories:
.TP
\fBFunction-local\fR
From declaration to the end of the enclosing block.
.TP
\fBProgram\fR
Namespace-level bindings, valid for the entire program.
.TP
\fBHeap\fR
From allocation to \fBfree\fR or \fBdestroy\fR.
.PP
Ownership is not enforced by the compiler. The following conventions are
used throughout the standard library:
.TP
\fB"Caller owns the returned memory"\fR
The function allocates; the caller releases.
.TP
\fB"Callee owns the memory"\fR
The function retains the reference; the caller must not release.
.TP
\fB"Borrowed reference"\fR
The function uses the memory without taking ownership. The caller keeps it
alive for the duration of the call.
.SH extern AND export
\fBextern\fR binds to a symbol defined in another object file:
.nf
    extern var some_global: u32;
    extern "c" var errno: c_int;
.fi
.PP
\fBexport\fR publishes a symbol with a C-ABI-compatible type:
.nf
    export var counter: u32 = 0;
    export fn get_counter() u32 { return counter; }
.fi
.PP
Pointer types in \fBextern\fR declarations must use C-compatible forms:
\fB[*c]T\fR for C pointers, \fB*T\fR for single-item pointers with known
alignment.
.SH VERSION NOTES
The following changes apply to Zig 0.17.0.
.SS Array multiplication removed
.nf
    var a = [1]u8{0} ** n;       // removed
    var a: [n]u8 = @splat(0);    // replacement
.fi
.SS void initialization
\fBvoid\fR values are written as \fB{}\fR. The forms \fBvoid{}\fR and
\fB.{}\fR for void are no longer accepted.
.SS @intFromEnum and @enumFromInt replaced
.nf
    @intFromEnum(e)     ->  @backingInt(e)
    @enumFromInt(x)     ->  @fromBackingInt(x)
.fi
.PP
The replacements also operate on packed structs and packed unions.
.SS @divCeil
Added. Computes ceiling division:
.nf
    const q = @divCeil(10, 3);   // 4
.fi
.SS @Vector in extern types
\fB@Vector\fR is no longer permitted in \fBextern\fR structs and unions, or
in parameters of functions with \fBcallconv(.winapi)\fR. Use an array field
with an accessor:
.nf
    const V = extern union {
        arr: [8]f32,
        pub fn vec(self: @This()) @Vector(8, f32) {
            return self.arr;
        }
    };
.fi
.SS @BitCast on extern structs
\fB@BitCast\fR rejects \fBextern\fR structs. Implement a userland bitcast:
.nf
    fn bitCast(T: type, v: anytype) T {
        const N = @sizeOf(T);
        if (@sizeOf(@TypeOf(v)) != N) @compileError("size mismatch");
        var out: T = undefined;
        @as(*[N]u8, @ptrCast(&out)).* =
            @as(*const [N]u8, @ptrCast(&v)).*;
        return out;
    }
.fi
.SH COMMON ERRORS
.TP
\fBlocal variable is never mutated\fR
Change \fBvar\fR to \fBconst\fR, or assign to \fB_\fR.
.TP
\fBexpected '=', found ';'\fR
Every declaration requires an initializer. Use \fBundefined\fR if the
value is not yet known.
.TP
\fBlocal variable shadows declaration of 'name'\fR
Rename one binding. Shadowing is forbidden.
.TP
\fBreturning address of local variable\fR
Return by value, return a heap allocation, or reference a namespace-level
binding.
.TP
\fBcannot assign to dereference of const pointer\fR
The pointee is immutable. Remove \fBconst\fR from the pointed-to type, or
restructure to avoid the write.
.TP
\fBthread local variables cannot be const\fR
Use \fBvar\fR for \fBthreadlocal\fR declarations.
